{"name":"Veltliner White","hex":"#d7d8c3","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"veltliner","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of Veltliner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/veltliner"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grüner Veltliner","blocks":[{"text":"Grüner Veltliner German: [ˈɡʁyːnɐ fɛltˈliːnɐ] ) is a white wine grape variety grown primarily in Austria, Hungary, Slovakia, and the Czech Republic. The leaves of the grape vine are five-lobed with bunches that are long but compact, and deep green grapes that ripen in mid-late October in the Northern Hemisphere."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Gruener_Veltliner_Weinsberg_20060909.jpg/330px-Gruener_Veltliner_Weinsberg_20060909.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gr%C3%BCner_Veltliner"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Pinot_Noir_Grapes_Ripening.jpg/500px-Pinot_Noir_Grapes_Ripening.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Pinot_Noir_Grapes_Ripening.jpg/500px-Pinot_Noir_Grapes_Ripening.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Through Savagnin, Grüner Veltliner is either a grandchild or a half-sibling of Pinot noir (pictured).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APinot_Noir_Grapes_Ripening.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Gruner_Veltliner_grapes_being_hand_harvested_at_Hahndorf_Hill_vineyard_in_the_Adelaide_Hills.jpg/500px-Gruner_Veltliner_grapes_being_hand_harvested_at_Hahndorf_Hill_vineyard_in_the_Adelaide_Hills.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Gruner_Veltliner_grapes_being_hand_harvested_at_Hahndorf_Hill_vineyard_in_the_Adelaide_Hills.jpg/500px-Gruner_Veltliner_grapes_being_hand_harvested_at_Hahndorf_Hill_vineyard_in_the_Adelaide_Hills.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Grüner Veltliner being hand harvested at Hahndorf Hill vineyard in the Adelaide Hills","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGruner_Veltliner_grapes_being_hand_harvested_at_Hahndorf_Hill_vineyard_in_the_Adelaide_Hills.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Vineyards_along_the_Danube_in_Wachau.jpg/500px-Vineyards_along_the_Danube_in_Wachau.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Vineyards_along_the_Danube_in_Wachau.jpg/1280px-Vineyards_along_the_Danube_in_Wachau.jpg?u